Keep-alive PING as quic-go schedules it (hysteria client).

quic-go sends a keep-alive PING at lastPacketReceivedTime + max(interval, 1.5 PTO), one per
silence, and only a received packet re-arms it (vendor/quic-go/connection.go:701-705, :877-885,
:1729-1731, :1779-1781). The PING is a queued frame, so it rides in the next packet whatever else
that packet carries.

Upstream ngtcp2 counts the timer from the last packet written or received on the path and puts a
PING only into an otherwise empty packet, so against a silent peer it keeps sending a PING every
interval, and an outgoing ACK moves the next one later.

This patch: the base moves only in conn_restart_timer_on_read (every processed packet, any space);
NGTCP2_CONN_FLAG_KEEP_ALIVE_PING_SENT holds further keep-alives until something arrives; the
expiry is asked of every 1-RTT packet and the PING is added to an ack-eliciting packet too.
The interval itself is set by src/quic/conn.c (keep_alive_interval).

--- a/lib/ngtcp2_conn.c
+++ b/lib/ngtcp2_conn.c
@@ -2163,6 +2163,14 @@
 static void conn_restart_timer_on_read(ngtcp2_conn *conn, ngtcp2_tstamp ts) {
   conn->idle_ts = ts;
   conn->flags |= NGTCP2_CONN_FLAG_RESTART_IDLE_TIMER_ON_WRITE;
+  /* The keep-alive timer counts from the last packet received and
+     from nothing else, as quic-go's nextKeepAliveTime counts from
+     lastPacketReceivedTime, which every processed packet sets and
+     which clears keepAlivePingSent (vendor/quic-go/connection.go:
+     871-877, :1726-1728, :1776-1778). */
+  conn->keep_alive.last_ts = ts;
+  conn->flags &= (uint32_t) ~(NGTCP2_CONN_FLAG_KEEP_ALIVE_CANCELLED |
+                              NGTCP2_CONN_FLAG_KEEP_ALIVE_PING_SENT);
 }
 
